1
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:04,680
In 1959, this was a trade.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,420
You apprenticed for years to do it. You could feed a family on it.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,020
And then, inside one working lifetime, it was gone.

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,400
Not automated. Not outsourced. Gone.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,880
So what happened to work?

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,900
Start here. San Francisco, 1906.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,420
A camera bolted to the front of a streetcar, pointed at an ordinary afternoon.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,600
Count the jobs.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,860
Every one of these men is selling the same thing: his back.

10
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,400
Teamster. Drayman. Newsboy. Crossing sweeper.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,300
The horse is the engine. The man is the controller.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,020
Out of frame, that logic runs the whole economy.

13
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:54,400
Lumber came down by hand.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,900
Rail went in by hand.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,580
Tunnels were dug by hand, by men standing in the dark.

16
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:06,400
Your body was your qualification.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,600
Then something inverts.

18
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:12,680
The machine stops being the tool.

19
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:16,240
It becomes the thing that sets the pace.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,900
1936. A Chevrolet body plant.

21
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,980
The film this comes from is called Master Hands — and it is almost entirely hands.

22
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:30,100
Faces barely appear.

23
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,700
Because the skill is no longer in the whole person. It's in one motion, repeated.

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,420
This buys something real: things get cheap, and fast, and the same every time.

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,880
And it costs something real.

26
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,460
1934. San Francisco. The whole city stops.

27
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,800
Not over machines. Over who the pace was set by.

28
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,080
This is the argument the twentieth century keeps having with itself.

29
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,900
It has never actually finished.

30
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,420
Meanwhile a different kind of job is growing, and nobody's counting it as labour.

31
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,300
1949. An instructional film teaching teenagers how to be employable.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,020
Its subject is not a skill. It's a surface.

33
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,680
Be clean. Be pressed. Be pleasant.

34
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,580
Because the new work is done in front of other people.

35
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,900
Hamtramck, Michigan. A beauty salon.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,420
This is a factory too. The product is how someone feels when they leave.

37
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,240
You can't stamp that out of sheet metal.

38
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,020
Which is exactly why these jobs are still here.

39
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,960
And why they were paid the least.

40
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,020
By the 1960s, a third kind of worker appears.

41
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,240
They don't lift anything. They don't serve anyone.

42
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,180
They watch an instrument and decide.

43
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,880
Air traffic control.

44
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,020
Nothing in this room tells you what's physically happening.

45
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,420
The job is to hold the real world in your head, from a screen.

46
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,700
And once work can happen on a screen, the screen starts taking jobs with it.

47
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,600
The projectionist.

48
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,600
The typesetter.

49
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,880
Two trades. Both skilled. Both gone inside forty years.

50
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,880
And neither was replaced by a robot.

51
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,180
They were replaced by a file.

52
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,740
That's the pattern, and it's the whole pattern.

53
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,140
Work doesn't disappear. It moves — out of the arms, into the hands,

54
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,960
out of the hands, into the attention.

55
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,580
Which raises the only question that actually matters about your job:

56
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,580
which part of it is the part a file can't hold?
